= Emergent algorithm =

An '''emergent algorithm''' is an informal term for a computational procedure in which useful global behavior arises from repeated local rules or interactions rather than from a central controller directly specifying every system-wide action. The designer specifies local mechanisms; the collective computation, pattern, or organization appears at a larger scale through their interaction.

The term is not a standardized algorithm class in the same sense as sorting algorithms, graph algorithms, or linear programming. It is used most defensibly for local distributed and self-organizing procedures whose correctness or performance is defined at the global level.

Examples include:

* local flocking rules producing coherent group motion;
* distributed averaging producing consensus;
* self-organizing particles producing a target shape;
* cellular automata producing propagating or computational structures;
* stigmergic agents producing trails or clustering.

“Emergent” should describe the relation between local specification and global outcome, not replace an explanation of how the algorithm works.

== Local rule, global property ==

A conventional centralized algorithm can directly maintain a global state and execute a prescribed sequence of operations.

An emergent algorithm instead has many local components.

A generic pattern is:

<pre>
local state
+ local observations
+ local update rule
→ repeated interactions
→ global pattern or computation
</pre>

The global property may never be stored explicitly in any one component.

For example, no single boid needs to know the flock's global shape for flock-like motion to emerge.

== Designed emergence ==

An emergent algorithm can be deliberately designed.

The fact that global behavior is not explicitly scripted step by step does not mean the system arose without engineering.

The designer can choose:

* state variables;
* topology;
* neighborhood;
* update rule;
* randomization;
* constraints;
* objective.

Emergence describes the route from local rules to global result.

It does not imply lack of design.

== Self-organization ==

[[Self-organization]] is closely related.

A self-organizing algorithm produces structured system-level states through local interaction without a central controller imposing the final arrangement.

Examples can include:

* aggregation;
* shape formation;
* leader election;
* distributed memory;
* consensus.

Self-organization is broader than emergent algorithms because self-organizing behavior also occurs in biological and physical systems without being an algorithm.

== Distributed algorithms ==

Many emergent algorithms are [[Distributed system|distributed algorithms]].

However, not every distributed algorithm is usefully called emergent.

A distributed database protocol can follow an explicit global specification with strong centralized coordination.

The emergent label is most informative when global organization arises indirectly from local rules and local information.

== Flocking ==

Craig Reynolds's 1987 boids model is a classic computational example.

Each simulated agent responds to nearby neighbors using local steering behavior.

The aggregate produces flock-like motion without a central flock controller.<ref name="reynolds">[https://doi.org/10.1145/37401.37406 Reynolds, C. W. (1987), “Flocks, Herds, and Schools: A Distributed Behavioral Model.”]</ref>

The local-to-global relationship is the important feature.

The model is not a full biological account of real flocking.

== Consensus ==

Consensus algorithms show how local exchange can produce global agreement.

Each node repeatedly adjusts its value using neighboring values.

Under appropriate graph and update conditions, the values converge.

No node needs the initial values of every other node.

Consensus is especially useful because the global property can often be proved mathematically rather than observed only through simulation.

== Cellular automata ==

A cellular automaton updates each cell according to a local rule.

Repeated updates can produce:

* stable patterns;
* oscillators;
* propagating structures;
* complex computation.

[[Conway's Game of Life]] is a famous example.

The local rule is simple and fully specified.

The resulting global structures can be difficult to predict from inspection of the rule alone.

== Programmable matter ==

Research on self-organizing particle systems provides a rigorous modern example.

The amoebot model represents large collections of simple particles with limited memory and local communication.

Researchers have designed distributed algorithms for:

* leader election;
* shape formation;
* coating;
* compression;
* reconfiguration.

The challenge is to prove that local asynchronous actions produce the desired global property.<ref name="amoebot">[https://labs.engineering.asu.edu/sops/amoebot/ Arizona State University, Self-Organizing Particle Systems, “Computing by Programmable Particles.”]</ref>

== Stochastic emergence ==

Some emergent algorithms use randomness deliberately.

A local random process can bias the system toward desirable macrostates.

For example, a Markov-chain particle algorithm can cause a distributed particle system to spend most of its long-run time in highly compressed configurations under suitable parameters.<ref name="compression">[https://jdaymude.github.io/publication/cannon2016-compression/ Cannon, Daymude, Randall and Richa (2016), “A Markov Chain Algorithm for Compression in Self-Organizing Particle Systems.”]</ref>

The system is stochastic locally but analyzable globally.

== Invariants ==

An '''invariant''' is a property preserved by every valid update.

Useful invariants can include:

* connectivity;
* total number of particles;
* conserved quantity;
* safety separation.

Proving invariants is one of the strongest ways to reason about emergent systems.

A visually convincing simulation cannot substitute for a proof of safety where safety matters.

== Convergence ==

A global target can be expressed as a set of acceptable configurations <math>G</math>.

An emergent algorithm should establish whether the system:

* reaches <math>G</math>;
* remains in <math>G</math>;
* approaches <math>G</math> probabilistically;
* oscillates among acceptable states.

Convergence can be deterministic or probabilistic.

The distinction must be explicit.

== Robustness ==

Local algorithms can be robust because they avoid a central point of control.

Robustness can include tolerance to:

* node failure;
* communication loss;
* asynchronous updates;
* local noise.

But local design does not guarantee robustness.

A single failed bridge node can partition a network.

Robustness must be tested or proved under a stated failure model.

== Asynchrony ==

Many self-organizing systems do not update every component at once.

An asynchronous scheduler activates one or several agents over time.

An algorithm proved only under synchronous rounds may fail when updates are delayed or interleaved.

The scheduler model is therefore part of the algorithm.

== Emergent behavior versus emergent algorithm ==

A system can display unplanned emergent behavior even when the algorithm was not designed to exploit it.

For example, independent trading agents can create unexpected market dynamics.

That is emergent behavior.

Calling it an emergent algorithm is stronger: the local computational procedure is being treated as a mechanism for producing a global function or property.

== Evolutionary algorithms ==

An '''evolutionary algorithm''' searches by selection, variation, and reproduction-inspired operators.

It can discover strategies the designer did not explicitly encode.

That is different from an emergent algorithm.

An emergent algorithm can be hand-designed.

An evolutionary algorithm can be centralized.

The concepts overlap only in some systems.

== Learned algorithms ==

Machine learning can produce policies or neural networks whose internal strategy was not explicitly programmed.

This is sometimes described as emergent computation.

Again, that is not sufficient to classify the result as an emergent algorithm.

The important distinction is whether global behavior is produced through interacting local components.

== Emergent strategy ==

Multi-agent systems can develop conventions or strategies not anticipated by the designer.

These are emergent strategies.

They can be beneficial or harmful.

A system should not call every surprising learned behavior an algorithm unless it has a stable operational procedure and defined function.

== Verification ==

Emergent algorithms can be difficult to verify because local correctness does not imply global correctness.

Verification methods include:

* mathematical proof;
* invariant analysis;
* model checking;
* Markov-chain analysis;
* simulation;
* randomized stress testing.

Proof is strongest where the model matches deployment.

Simulation is essential when the full system is too complex for exact analysis.

== Simulation ==

A simulation should vary:

* initial conditions;
* random seeds;
* update schedules;
* network size;
* communication delays;
* failures.

A single visually attractive run provides weak evidence.

Emergent behavior often has multiple basins of attraction.

== Scaling ==

A local rule that works for 100 agents may fail for 100,000.

Scaling can change:

* interaction density;
* message load;
* convergence time;
* failure probability.

Complexity should therefore be measured as system size grows.

== Safety ==

Emergence is not inherently desirable.

Local rules can produce:

* congestion;
* runaway feedback;
* oscillation;
* fragmentation;
* deadlock.

Safety constraints should be proved independently of performance when possible.

== Engineering workflow ==

A useful design workflow is:

# define global objective;
# identify local information available;
# design local rule;
# specify scheduler and failure model;
# prove invariants;
# analyze convergence;
# simulate edge cases;
# test physical or deployed implementation.

This converts “emergence” from a metaphor into an engineering method.

== Epistemic boundary ==

The term emergent algorithm is useful but informal.

A rigorous article should always replace the label with a precise statement of:

* local state;
* neighborhood;
* update rule;
* global target;
* convergence conditions.

If those are absent, “emergent” explains very little.

